WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.438
Preference optimization usually asks one objective to supervise an entire answer.

2
00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:07.680
TFPO starts from a different premise:

3
00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:11.791
different tokens play different roles, so they should not all receive

4
00:00:11.791 --> 00:00:13.286
the same training signal.

5
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:19.264
Reasoning tokens determine whether an explanation is helpful and well justified.

6
00:00:19.264 --> 00:00:22.151
Final answers, option letters, schema keys,

7
00:00:22.151 --> 00:00:27.924
and code fragments often need likelihood anchoring to remain stable and parseable.

8
00:00:27.924 --> 00:00:33.401
Applying one sequence-level preference signal everywhere can make these roles interfere.

9
00:00:34.183 --> 00:00:38.127
TFPO adds a lightweight gate to every response token.

10
00:00:38.127 --> 00:00:42.715
Low gate values send more weight to a DPO-style preference objective.

11
00:00:42.715 --> 00:00:45.988
High values emphasize chosen-response likelihood.

12
00:00:45.988 --> 00:00:50.117
In effect, reasoning-sensitive spans receive preference shaping,

13
00:00:50.117 --> 00:00:53.656
while answer-bearing spans receive stronger anchoring.

14
00:00:54.417 --> 00:00:59.611
The route is learned directly from preference pairs, without token-level supervision.

15
00:00:59.611 --> 00:01:02.725
3 regularizers keep the gate useful:

16
00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:05.839
a ratio target prevents branch collapse,

17
00:01:05.839 --> 00:01:11.029
total variation discourages noisy switching, and entropy prevents premature saturation.

18
00:01:11.817 --> 00:01:19.706
Under one frozen 10-benchmark protocol, TFPO averages 88.00 on post-trained Qwen3-8B.

19
00:01:19.706 --> 00:01:28.313
SimPO reaches 83.08, and the full-response SimPO plus NLL control reaches 83.52.

20
00:01:28.313 --> 00:01:32.591
TFPO is strongest on every benchmark in the main table.

21
00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:37.598
The routing claim is tested independently of benchmark accuracy.

22
00:01:37.598 --> 00:01:44.723
On 1,000 blind annotated responses, the content gate reaches 0.85 AUPRC,

23
00:01:44.723 --> 00:01:49.905
versus 0.55 for the strongest strict position-only control.

24
00:01:49.905 --> 00:01:52.693
Moving the answer to the beginning,

25
00:01:52.693 --> 00:01:56.876
middle, or end changes TFPO's score by only 0.02.

26
00:01:57.683 --> 00:02:02.608
The same routing recipe improves open-ended alignment across Qwen,

27
00:02:02.608 --> 00:02:04.797
Llama, and Mistral backbones.

28
00:02:04.797 --> 00:02:09.383
TFPO is strongest in every reported AlpacaEval and Arena-Hard column,

29
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:13.969
suggesting that the gain is not tied to one tokenizer

30
00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:15.803
or one instruction-tuning recipe.

31
00:02:16.583 --> 00:02:23.379
With a Qwen3-VL backbone, TFPO reaches the best multimodal average of

32
00:02:23.379 --> 00:02:25.850
71.03 across 10 benchmarks.

33
00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.292
The qualitative cases show 3 distinct instabilities:

34
00:02:29.292 --> 00:02:35.191
omitting a required option letter, selecting an unsupported answer from a map,

35
00:02:35.191 --> 00:02:38.633
and producing an unnecessarily long numerical conclusion.

36
00:02:39.417 --> 00:02:43.088
Across 4 matched samples per prompt,

37
00:02:43.088 --> 00:02:46.759
TFPO raises answer agreement to 92.69,

38
00:02:46.759 --> 00:02:52.266
majority accuracy to 86.32, and average accuracy to 90.57.

39
00:02:52.266 --> 00:02:59.019
Explanation diversity also rises to 62.33 rather than being traded away.

40
00:02:59.783 --> 00:03:01.743
The central lesson is simple.

41
00:03:01.743 --> 00:03:04.887
It is not enough to ask which objectives should be mixed.

42
00:03:04.887 --> 00:03:08.908
We should also ask where each objective should act inside the response.

43
00:03:08.908 --> 00:03:13.367
TFPO turns that question into a learned, token-level route.
